  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Research indexed lookup with minimal other changes. Replace repeated find_by_id scans with an immutable, collision-safe integer-to-row hash table sized from the actual input. Preserve first-row lookup semantics, support arbitrary integer IDs, and distinguish missing entries from valid IDs. Initially retain the existing traversal and arithmetic to isolate the indexing benefit. Compare against the baseline; consider a small-input linear fallback only if measurements justify a general size-based crossover. Do not assume dense IDs, sorted input, or topological numbering. Deliver a complete validated candidate, measured timings, memory costs, and an interface G can reuse.",
        "B": "Research lazy memoization and stack-safe traversal. Cache each reached row's selected successor and final float result. Follow successors using an explicit path stack until reaching a terminal or cached row, then unwind with the original value-plus-child operation, rounding to float at every node. A terminal must retain its original value without adding an artificial zero. Reuse results across all queries while preserving their output order and spelling. Resolve only reached rows rather than eagerly processing the entire graph. Keep ID lookup separable for integration with A. Measure the standalone benefit; shared-path overlap is a hypothesis, not an observed property.",
        "C": "Research input and representation overhead. Use buffered, line-aware parsing into compact node arrays and contiguous ordered integer adjacency, eliminating fixed 100-byte neighbor storage and repeated string copying/tokenization. Preserve source-compatible token boundaries, raw query-token spelling, and float conversion equivalent to scanf's %f; avoid approximate decimal parsing. Benchmark a serial implementation first. As a secondary experiment, only if the unchanged build supports threading, compare serial successor selection with at most four workers selecting successors for disjoint rows against immutable lookup data. Preserve neighbor-order ties, avoid shared memoization races, and retain a general workload-based serial fallback. Do not assume eager preprocessing or threading will win.",
        "G": "Integrate only complete, validated programs produced by A/B/C. Keep the baseline and best standalone candidate available. Prioritize a measured A+B combination, then assess C's representation/parser changes incrementally; add eager or parallel selection only when its integrated measurements justify it. Recheck correctness after every integration, including greedy selection, missing IDs, ties, nonpositive values, sentinel handling, query formatting, and float evaluation order. Use the unchanged harness, oracle, compiler, target, and budgets. Compare actual combined timings rather than adding estimated gains, and account for the baseline's timing spread. Select the best measured valid program, even if it is an unmerged candidate.",
        "analysis": "The supplied target is Problem F, src/graph.c. The only measurement is a baseline correct on four cases: mean 13.095 ms, samples 12.750–13.359 ms. There is no profile or input-size evidence. The source exposes three plausible costs: repeated linear ID lookup, repeated traversal of shared suffixes, and string parsing/storage. Correctness requires following the first listed existing neighbor whose raw float value strictly exceeds the running maximum, initially zero—not maximizing downstream path sums. Missing queried IDs return zero; missing neighbors are ignored. Preserve first-match ID lookup, the original selected-ID -1 no-recursion sentinel, query-token spelling/order, and printf formatting. Preserve child-first float additions with per-node rounding; forward accumulation or reassociation can change results. Input order and numeric IDs need not be topological. Four ARM64 cores are available, but this short baseline makes low-overhead serial improvements the priority; startup, I/O, and threading costs remain unmeasured."
      },
